/annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 128.49 -23.62 C 128.49 -17.89 123.84 -13.24 118.11 -13.24 C 112.38 -13.24 107.73 -17.89 107.73 -23.62 C 107.73 -29.35 112.38 -34 118.11 -34 C 123.84 -34 128.49 -29.35 128.49 -23.62 Z M 118.11 -23.62"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.6 0.0 0.0 0.6 116.04 -26.51)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.06em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F2.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 128.46 20.85 C 146.71 15.96 157.7 4.98 161.3 -8.47"></path><g transform="matrix(0.25882 -0.96593 0.96593 0.25882 161.3 -8.47)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.42426 -0.42426 0.42426 0.42426 151.05 12.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.57em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="7.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F2.pic1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 175.7 -20.85 C 192.41 -16.37 192.41 -30.87 180.51 -27.68"></path><g transform="matrix(-0.96593 0.25883 -0.25883 -0.96593 180.51 -27.68)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.0 -0.6 0.6 0.0 181.56 -21.25)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.57em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="7.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F2.pic1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 154.65 -23.62 L 133.8 -23.62"></path><g transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 133.8 -23.62)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.6 0.0 0.0 0.6 135.9 -30.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="19.44"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F2.pic1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 115.34 -13.28 C 110.86 3.43 125.36 3.43 122.17 -8.47"></path><g transform="matrix(-0.25879 -0.96593 0.96593 -0.25879 122.17 -8.47)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.6 0.0 0.0 0.6 108.81 2.35)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.24em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="31"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F2.pic1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="True" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">True</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Automaton corresponding to a syntax <span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“if <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, do <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, else <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>”</span> (left) and <span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“while <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, do <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>”</span> (right). </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-Based Plan to Automaton: </span>
To formally verify a plan against logic specifications, we need to express the plan in a formal representation such as an FSA. Existing works have developed methods for converting natural language or programming language to FSA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We use an algorithm, denoted as <span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">L2A</span>, that takes a text-based plan <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, expressed in natural language or programming language, as input and converts it into an FSA <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\texttt{L2A}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">L2A</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\texttt{L2A}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the algorithm parses the text input into phrases (e.g., keywords and variables in a programming language). It then follows pre-defined grammar to convert the parsed phrases into automaton states and transitions.
We present an automaton conversion example in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Preliminaries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and present the details in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B L2A: Text to Automaton" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.26935v1/pipeline.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:572/322;" width="572" height="322" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>An illustration for collection of auto-labeled training data for the projector, training the projector to construct a safety-separable latent space, and predicting plan compliance against natural language rules via the spatial location of the projector’s output representation.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>RepV: A Neurosymbolic Verifier</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> is a neurosymbolic verifier that unifies formal verification with representation learning to <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">enable verification of externally generated action plans against natural language rules</span>. At its core, <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> constructs a latent space where safe and unsafe plans are linearly separable, enabling efficient inference of compliance with natural-language rules. This latent-space embedding bridges the symbolic rigor with linguistic accessibility, establishing a foundation to extend verification toward probabilistic guarantees and iterative refinement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Components: </span>
<span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> comprises four main components: an external knowledge source for plan generation, an interpreter <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a projector <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a model checker <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">- Knowledge Source (Planner): </span> An external source used for plan generation. In this setting, we use a <em id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multimodal foundation model</em> that accepts text- or image-based task prompts as input, generating a text-based plan that fulfills the task. Note that the <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rule</span> is a part of the task prompt as well. For brevity, we denote it as a <em id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">planner</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">- Interpreter: </span> A language model <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> that processes a generated plan <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and a natural language rule <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing a binary classification <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating its prediction on rule compliance (1 means comply) and a text-based rationale <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Mathematically, we have <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y,\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p,r_{nl})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y,\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p,r_{nl})</annotation></semantics></math>. An illustrative example of interpreter outputs is in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Demonstration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">- Projector: </span> A multi-layer perception <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}:\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}:\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> that takes the <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <em id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">text embeddings</em> <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emb(p,\mathcal{R})\in\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emb(p,\mathcal{R})\in\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from the plan and the interpreter’s rationale and maps them into an <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional latent space, where compliance and noncompliance can be distinguished spatially.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">- Model Checker: </span> A formal verification tool <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> verifies whether a plan <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, represented as a finite-state automaton <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{p}=\texttt{L2A}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">L2A</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{p}=\texttt{L2A}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies a logical specification <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> when executed in a world/system model <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TS</annotation></semantics></math>.
It returns a binary ground-truth label indicating compliance:</p>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\quad\mathcal{M_{C}}(\mathcal{A}_{p},\phi)=1\text{ if }\mathcal{A}_{p}\otimes TS\models\phi,\text{ else }0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>⊧</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext> else </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\quad\mathcal{M_{C}}(\mathcal{A}_{p},\phi)=1\text{ if }\mathcal{A}_{p}\otimes TS\models\phi,\text{ else }0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition of Safety: </span>
To learn a safety-separable latent space, we first define the terminology on <em id="S4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safe</em> and <em id="S4.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unsafe</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1:</span>
Given a plan <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, a natural language rule <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a logical specification <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> that corresponds to <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math> describes the semantic meaning of <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>), we obtain a natural language interpretation <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y,\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p,r_{nl})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y,\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p,r_{nl})</annotation></semantics></math> and the formal verification outcome <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}=\mathcal{M_{C}}(\mathcal{A}_{p},\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}=\mathcal{M_{C}}(\mathcal{A}_{p},\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We define the plan <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as <span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">safe</span> with respect to the rule <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if its predicted classification aligns with the formal verification outcome:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\text{ is safe }\iff y=y^{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is safe </mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\text{ is safe }\iff y=y^{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the “safety” in <span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> differs from the everyday notion of physical or behavioral safety. A plan is considered safe even if it violates the rule (<math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), as long as the interpreter’s prediction correctly reflects that violation (<math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math>).
Conversely, a plan is unsafe when there is a misalignment between the interpreter’s prediction and the verified outcome (<math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\neq y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\neq y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>), regardless of whether the plan itself satisfies or violates the rule.
Hence, safety in this context measures <em id="S4.p10.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic consistency between reasoning modalities</em>—the alignment of linguistic interpretation and formal verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Statement: </span> Given a plan <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from external sources (e.g., AI-generated) and a natural language rule <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">verify</span> whether each plan complies with the rule, denoted as <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\models r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⊧</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\models r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math>, and estimate the <span id="S4.p11.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">probability</span> of such verification outcome being correct.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Learning a Safety-Separable Latent Space</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve reliable verification across linguistic and formal modalities, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> learns a latent space where semantically <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safe</em> and <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unsafe</em> plans become linearly separable. This latent space serves as a bridge between linguistic reasoning (from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and symbolic verification (from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>), enabling the system to infer compliance for new natural-language rules based on spatial proximity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Data Collection:</span>
We derive each training sample from a triplet <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,r_{nl},\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,r_{nl},\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a generated plan, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math> is a natural-language rule, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is its corresponding logical specification. The interpreter <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts a compliance label <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and generates a rationale <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y,\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p,r_{nl})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y,\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p,r_{nl}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We then convert the plan <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> into an automaton <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{p}=\texttt{L2A}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">L2A</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{p}=\texttt{L2A}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> and verify it against <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> by the model checker <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing a ground-truth label <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}=\mathcal{M_{C}}(\mathcal{A}_{p},\phi)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}=\mathcal{M_{C}}(\mathcal{A}_{p},\phi).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latent Representation:</span>
We obtain a text embedding <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emb(p,\mathcal{R})\in\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emb(p,\mathcal{R})\in\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> using a pretrained text encoder <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emb()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emb()</annotation></semantics></math> that encodes the plan <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and the interpreter’s rationale <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A lightweight projector <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}:\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}:\mathbb{R}^{\epsilon}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> maps this embedding into a low-dimensional latent representation <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\mathcal{P}(emb(p,\mathcal{R}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\mathcal{P}(emb(p,\mathcal{R}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Each representation <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a binary label <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{safe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{safe}</annotation></semantics></math> that indicates whether the interpreter’s prediction aligns with the verified outcome:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{safe}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;y=y^{*}\quad\text{(safe)},\\
0,&amp;y\neq y^{*}\quad\text{(unsafe)}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(safe)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(unsafe)</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{safe}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;y=y^{*}\quad\text{(safe)},\\
0,&amp;y\neq y^{*}\quad\text{(unsafe)}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Projector Optimization:</span>
We then train the projector using a cross-entropy loss that minimizes the classification error between the predicted safety <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{safe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{safe}</annotation></semantics></math> (predicted via the spatial location in the latent space) and the verified label <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{safe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{safe}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{proj}=-\sum_{i}y_{safe}^{(i)}\log\hat{y}_{safe}^{(i)}+(1-y_{safe}^{(i)})\log(1-\hat{y}_{safe}^{(i)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{proj}=-\sum_{i}y_{safe}^{(i)}\log\hat{y}_{safe}^{(i)}+(1-y_{safe}^{(i)})\log(1-\hat{y}_{safe}^{(i)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Through supervised learning, <span id="S4.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> learns a safety-separable latent space <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where spatial clusters encode alignment between linguistic interpretation and formal verification. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Preliminaries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the process involves collecting formally verified samples, projecting them into <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and learning a linear boundary that partitions safe and unsafe representations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.26935v1/pipeline-refine.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:572/208;" width="572" height="208" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Overview of the probabilistic verification and refinement pipeline. <span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> After training the latent space, we extract a calibration distribution <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> using distances from correctly verified samples to their cluster centroids. <span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Given a new plan–rule pair, we compute the distance between its latent representation and the nearest centroid <math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we use <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute the probabilistic guarantee. The guarantee can be subsequently used as a feedback signal for upgrading the planner.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Probabilistic Rule Verification</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the latent space <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> infers compliance for new plans and rules by projecting them into <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and estimating a probabilistic guarantee that reflects the probability of correct verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference Procedure:</span>
Given a new plan <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and a natural language rule <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}_{nl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}_{nl}</annotation></semantics></math>, the interpreter <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a binary classification and rationale:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime},\mathcal{R}^{\prime}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p^{\prime},r^{\prime}_{nl})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime},\mathcal{R}^{\prime}=\mathcal{M_{I}}(p^{\prime},r^{\prime}_{nl}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the projector maps the text embedding into the latent space:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}=\mathcal{P}(emb(p^{\prime},\mathcal{R}^{\prime}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}=\mathcal{P}(emb(p^{\prime},\mathcal{R}^{\prime})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We then attach a linear classifier <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathbb{R}^{m}\rightarrow\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the compliance label:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}^{\prime}_{safe}=C(z^{\prime})\in\{0,1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}^{\prime}_{safe}=C(z^{\prime})\in\{0,1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the interpreter’s reasoning is aligned with the expected formal outcome.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Calibration Dataset:</span>
To provide a probability estimate for this inference, we maintain a small calibration set <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{c}=\{(z_{i},y_{i},y_{i}^{*})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{c}=\{(z_{i},y_{i},y_{i}^{*})\}</annotation></semantics></math> that is <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.i.d.</em> with the potential testing set, where each <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}=\mathcal{P}(emb(p_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}=\mathcal{P}(emb(p_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> is a latent representation and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the formal verification result from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M_{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M_{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each correctly classified sample (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=y_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=y_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>), we compute its distance to the centroid <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of the corresponding cluster:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2},\quad c^{*}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{z_{j}\in\mathcal{S}}z_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2},\quad c^{*}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{z_{j}\in\mathcal{S}}z_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of correctly aligned (safe) samples.
These distances form a calibration distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> that captures how far safe samples typically lie from their centroid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distance-Based Probabilistic Guarantee:</span>
For a new inference <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p^{\prime},r^{\prime}_{nl})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p^{\prime},r^{\prime}_{nl})</annotation></semantics></math> with latent representation <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute its distance to the nearest centroid. We then formulate a calibration distribution that captures the relation between the distances and prediction accuracies. This distribution is used to estimate the probability that a prediction <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is correct, denoted as the <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">probabilistic guarantee</em> <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2 (Distance to Centroid):</span>

Let <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> be the centroids of the safe and unsafe clusters in <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a new input <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p^{\prime},r^{\prime}_{nl})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p^{\prime},r^{\prime}_{nl})</annotation></semantics></math> with embedding <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the distance to its nearest centroid is</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=\min\big(\|z^{\prime}-c_{s}\|_{2},\,\|z^{\prime}-c_{u}\|_{2}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=\min\big(\|z^{\prime}-c_{s}\|_{2},\,\|z^{\prime}-c_{u}\|_{2}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3 (Calibration Distribution):</span>

Let <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}:\mathbb{R}^{+}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}:\mathbb{R}^{+}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the cumulative distribution of distances from correctly classified samples to their cluster centroids. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{c}^{*}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{c}^{*}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the safety label associated with <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a new input, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}(d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}(d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> gives the probability that a sample from the opposite class lies beyond distance <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}(d^{\prime})=\Pr\big[\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}&gt;d^{\prime}\,\big|\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}(d^{\prime})=\Pr\big[\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}&gt;d^{\prime}\,\big|\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">If the safe and unsafe classes are imbalanced, we compute <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> separately for each class and select the corresponding <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the nearest centroid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1 (Probabilistic Guarantee):</span>

Given a new input with latent representation <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and nearest centroid <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}\in\{c_{s},c_{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}\in\{c_{s},c_{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the probabilistic guarantee <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> that its classification is correct is</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})=1-\frac{(1-F_{C}(d^{\prime}))\cdot\Pr[y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})=1-\frac{(1-F_{C}(d^{\prime}))\cdot\Pr[y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof 1:</span>
Let <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}\in\{c_{s},c_{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}\in\{c_{s},c_{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the nearest centroid of the new input’s latent representation <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=\|z^{\prime}-c^{*}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=\|z^{\prime}-c^{*}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Definition 3, the calibration distribution satisfies</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}(d^{\prime})=\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}&gt;d^{\prime}\mid y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}(d^{\prime})=\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}&gt;d^{\prime}\mid y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p">We define the probabilistic guarantee as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Pr[\,y_{i}=y_{c}^{*}\mid\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Pr[\,y_{i}=y_{c}^{*}\mid\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\mid\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\mid\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.3" class="ltx_p">Applying Bayes’ rule,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mid\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mid\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\mid y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\mid y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{(1-F_{C}(d^{\prime}))\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{(1-F_{C}(d^{\prime}))\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.4" class="ltx_p">Substituting into the definition of <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})=1-\frac{(1-F_{C}(d^{\prime}))\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})=1-\frac{(1-F_{C}(d^{\prime}))\Pr[\,y_{i}\neq y_{c}^{*}\,]}{\Pr[\,\|z_{i}-c^{*}\|_{2}\leq d^{\prime}\,]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.5" class="ltx_p">Hence proven.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification:</span>
<math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes uncertainty associated with the rule compliance prediction. A higher <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> implies stronger alignment between linguistic interpretation and formal verification.
Formally, a new plan <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">complies</span> with a natural-language rule <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{nl}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{nl}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with a <span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">guarantee</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p^{\prime}\models r_{nl}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊧</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p^{\prime}\models r_{nl}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\iff" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\iff</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\big[(\hat{y}^{\prime}_{safe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\big[(\hat{y}^{\prime}_{safe}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=1\wedge y^{\prime}=1)\ \lor\ (\hat{y}^{\prime}_{safe}=0\wedge y^{\prime}=0)\big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∧</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.722em">∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∧</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1\wedge y^{\prime}=1)\ \lor\ (\hat{y}^{\prime}_{safe}=0\wedge y^{\prime}=0)\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Guarantee-Driven Refinement</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To close the loop between verification and planning, <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> uses the probabilistic guarantee <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math> as a feedback signal to refine the planner, which is a foundation model that produces plans. We adopt two complementary strategies: supervised fine-tuning and preference optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) via Guarantee Filtering:</span>
We collect a dataset of plan–rule pairs <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,r_{nl})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,r_{nl})</annotation></semantics></math> along with their interpreter predictions and latent probabilities. We then filter this dataset to retain only high-confidence examples, i.e. those where the probabilistic guarantee exceeds a threshold <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9</annotation></semantics></math>). Formally, define</p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{hf}}=\{\,(p,r_{nl}):\hat{p}(y\mid z)\geq\tau\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>hf</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{hf}}=\{\,(p,r_{nl}):\hat{p}(y\mid z)\geq\tau\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{hf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>hf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{hf}}</annotation></semantics></math> to fine-tune the planner via cross-entropy loss, training it to generate plans that are rule compliant. Because the filtered set excludes high-uncertainty examples, the fine-tuning process focuses on reinforcing desired patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probabilistic Guarantee for Preference Ranking:</span>
We can use the probabilistic guarantee <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math> also as a measure of relative preference between alternative plans generated by the planner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For each given task, the planner generates two different plans <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by varying the random seed. Then, we estimate the guarantee for both plans: <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y_{1}\mid z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y_{1}\mid z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y_{2}\mid z_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y_{2}\mid z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We select the plan with <span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">higher guarantee of rule compliance</span> as the <em id="S5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">preferred output</em>, and the other as the <em id="S5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-preferred output</em>. Lastly, we add this triplet (task prompt, preferred output, non-preferred output) into the fine-tuning dataset <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pr</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pr}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We then directly pass <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pr</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pr}}</annotation></semantics></math> into a <em id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Direct Preference Optimization</em> (DPO) framework to fine-tune the planner. Compared to supervised fine-tuning, preference optimization performs better when multiple correct answers exist.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Demonstration</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> on navigation tasks using the Carla simulator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al. (2017)</a></cite>.
In this section, we (1) present our experimental setting, (2) visualize the safety-separable latent space and its corresponding calibration distribution, and (3) present case studies that highlight its verification capability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.26935v1/figure/example/prompt_1.jpg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:323/182;" width="323" height="182" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2510.26935v1/figure/example/prompt_2.jpg" id="S6.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:323/182;" width="323" height="182" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Example visual and textual input prompts to the planner.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup: </span>
We design a series of navigation tasks, such as <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">go straight</span>, <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">turn left</span>, <span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">turn right</span>, <span id="S6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">park</span>, and <span id="S6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">make a U-turn at the traffic light or the stop sign intersection</span>. We use <span id="S6.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-o1-mini</span> as the interpreter and a <span id="S6.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NuSMV</span> model checker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cimatti et al. (2002)</a></cite>. The projector comprises of three fully connected layers that map 1536-dimensional text embeddings (obtained from <span id="S6.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text-embedding-3-small</span>) to a 10-dimensional latent space (<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=10</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a set of pre-implemented APIs, such as:</p>
<div id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F5F5F5;--ltx-border-color:#C8C8C8;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,IyBQdWJsaXNoZXMgbGluZWFyIGFuZCBhbmd1bGFyIHZlbG9jaXR5IHRvIHRoZSBhZ2VudC4KdmVsb2NpdHlfcHVibGlzaGVyKGxpbmVhciwgYW5ndWxhcikKc3RvcCgpICMgU3RvcHMgdGhlIHJvYm90LgojIFJldHVybnMgYSBib29sZWFuIHZhbHVlLgpwZWRlc3RyaWFuX29ic2VydmVkKCk=" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">#<span id="lstnumberx1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Publishes<span id="lstnumberx1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>linear<span id="lstnumberx1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>and<span id="lstnumberx1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>angular<span id="lstnumberx1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>velocity<span id="lstnumberx1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>to<span id="lstnumberx1.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx1.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>agent.</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">velocity_publisher</span><span id="lstnumberx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">linear</span><span id="lstnumberx2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span><span id="lstnumberx2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">angular</span><span id="lstnumberx2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">)</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">stop</span><span id="lstnumberx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">()</span><span id="lstnumberx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">#<span id="lstnumberx3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Stops<span id="lstnumberx3.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx3.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>robot.</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">#<span id="lstnumberx4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Returns<span id="lstnumberx4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>a<span id="lstnumberx4.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>boolean<span id="lstnumberx4.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>value.</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">pedestrian_observed</span><span id="lstnumberx5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">()</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.26935v1/figure/plots/latent_untrained.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:162/141;" width="162" height="141" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.26935v1/figure/plots/latent_trained.png" id="S6.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:162/141;" width="162" height="141" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.26935v1/figure/plots/latent_test.png" id="S6.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:162/141;" width="162" height="141" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4"><img src="2510.26935v1/figure/plots/calib_dist.png" id="S6.F6.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:162/144;" width="162" height="144" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>The figures from left to right show: 1) the latent space before projector training, where safe and unsafe plans are inseparable, 2) latent space after training, which is separable by a linear decision boundary, 3) representations of <span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">out-of-domain</span> testing samples with <span id="S6.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">new rules</span>, and 4) calibration distribution <math id="S6.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from a calibration set that drawn from the same distribution with the training samples.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">we use <span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1-mini</span> as our planner to generate high-level plans composed of sequences of the system APIs. Specifically, the planner takes a text-based and/or image-based task prompt and the system APIs as inputs, producing a high-level plan, i.e., a Python program, that calls the APIs to accomplish the task. We show examples of input prompts in Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6 Demonstration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and output plans in Listing <a href="#LST1" title="Listing 1 ‣ 6 Demonstration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training: </span>
We generate 400 plans and 15 natural-language rules, each paired with its formal logic specification, to train the projector. For example, the rule <span id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Yield to pedestrian”</span> corresponds to</p>
<table id="S6.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}=\mathbf{G}\,\text{pedestrian}\rightarrow\mathbf{X}\neg\,\text{publish velocity}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>pedestrian</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">¬</mo><mtext>publish velocity</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}=\mathbf{G}\,\text{pedestrian}\rightarrow\mathbf{X}\neg\,\text{publish velocity}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The complete rules are in Listing <a href="#LST2" title="Listing 2 ‣ C.3 Natural Language Rules ‣ Appendix C Empirical Study on Navigation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Natural Language Rules ‣ Appendix C Empirical Study on Navigation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For each plan, we randomly sample one rule from the 15-rule set and query the interpreter to predict compliance and provide an explanation. Then, we use the model checker to obtain the ground-truth compliance label by verifying the corresponding formal specification. Each compliance prediction and rationale generation by the interpreter takes less than 3 seconds, while the model checking requires less than 0.1 seconds per plan.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">After collecting 400 labeled samples, we train the projector, which contains <span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">197 K parameters</span>, to predict rule compliance using cross-entropy loss with a batch size of 20 over 10 epochs. The full training process completes in <span id="S6.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">144 milliseconds</span>.
Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6 Demonstration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the latent spaces before and after training, demonstrating how the learned space achieves linear separability between safe and unsafe plans.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Calibration: </span>
We construct a calibration set of 400 additional samples drawn from the same distribution as the training set, using the same set of natural-language rules and their corresponding formal specifications.
Each sample is passed through the trained projector to obtain its latent representation and distance to the nearest cluster centroid. These distances form the calibration distribution <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This calibration process completes in only <span id="S6.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">225 milliseconds.</span>
We show the resulting distribution in the right plot of Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6 Demonstration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
