</div>
<div id="lstnumberx11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter">      </span><span id="lstnumberx11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000B4;">else</span><span id="lstnumberx11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">:</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter">        </span><span id="lstnumberx12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">velocity_publisher</span><span id="lstnumberx12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx12.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">speed</span><span id="lstnumberx12.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span><span id="lstnumberx12.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx12.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">angle</span><span id="lstnumberx12.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">)</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter">        </span><span id="lstnumberx13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">dist</span><span id="lstnumberx13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">=</span><span id="lstnumberx13.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx13.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">speed</span><span id="lstnumberx13.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx13.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">*</span><span id="lstnumberx13.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx13.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx13.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">time</span><span id="lstnumberx13.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx13.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">time</span><span id="lstnumberx13.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">()</span><span id="lstnumberx13.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx13.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">-</span><span id="lstnumberx13.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx13.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx13.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">)</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter">    </span><span id="lstnumberx14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">......</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F5F5F5;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float">Listing 1: </span>A plan for the task: “go across this intersection.”</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Given the meta prompt to the interpreter (including the rule): <span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Does the code meet the rule: Always avoiding pedestrians?”</span> the interpreter classifies the plan as violating the rule (<math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) with an explanation:
<span id="S6.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“It partially meets the rule. It stops when a pedestrian is detected during turning and completes the turn if unobstructed.”</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we obtain the latent representation of this plan <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The distances of <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to the centroids of the safe and unsafe clusters are <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.2517,\,1.0975]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1.2517</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1.0975</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.2517,\,1.0975]</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that the sample lies closer to the unsafe cluster.
The corresponding probabilistic guarantee is <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})=0.971" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.971</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}(y^{\prime}\mid z^{\prime})=0.971</annotation></semantics></math>, suggesting that <span id="S6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> is confident that the interpreter’s prediction <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is incorrect. This inference procedure takes an average of <span id="S6.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.5 seconds</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, we conclude that the plan has a 97.1% chance of satisfying the rule. To validate this conclusion, we convert the plan into an automaton (left in Figure <a href="#S6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 6 Demonstration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and verify it against <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model checker confirms that the plan satisfies the specification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p">This case highlights how <span id="S6.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> detects interpreter misclassifications. By leveraging the geometric separation and quantifying uncertainty in the latent space, <span id="S6.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> can rectify the language model interpreter’s misclassification, demonstrating its ability to provide reliable, uncertainty-aware verification against natural-language rules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Quantitative Analysis</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We quantitatively evaluate <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RepV</span> across multiple robotic platforms to assess its verification accuracy and impact on downstream planning performance, e.g., how the verification outcome guides the planner refinement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.26935v1/figure/plots/compliance-acc.png" id="S7.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:673/202;" width="673" height="202" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Comparison of three rule compliance prediction methods over four robot planning domains. RepV consistently achieves the highest accuracy compared to the two baselines. The error bar shows the standard deviation across five repetitive runs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S7.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Fine-tuning Statistics. Acronyms: BS = Batch Size, LR = Learning Rate Multiplier, E = Epochs, TD = Training Duration (min), TT = Trained Tokens, DS = Data Size (KB), S = Number of Samples, S/E = Steps per Epoch, Steps = Total Steps, CE = Convergence Epoch, CStep = Convergence Step. We define convergence if the training compliance rate fluctuates by less than 1% in the later epochs/steps. Our fine-tuning strategies <span id="S7.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">require fewer training samples and converge faster</span> than the baselines.</figcaption>
